---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import GuideCard from '../../components/GuideCard.astro';

const guides = (await getCollection('reliableRecallGuides')).sort((a,b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const schema = {
  '@context':'https://schema.org',
  '@type':'CollectionPage',
  name:'Dog Recall Training Guides',
  description:'Dog recall training guides for long-line work, distractions, wildlife, sniffing, other dogs and off-leash readiness.',
  url:'https://mercerlanepress.com/reliable-recall/',
  hasPart:guides.map(g => ({'@type':'Article',name:g.data.title,url:'https://mercerlanepress.com/reliable-recall/' + g.id + '/'}))
};
---
<BaseLayout title="Dog Recall Training: Long-Line & Real-World Recall" description="Dog recall training guides for long-line work, distractions, wildlife, sniffing, other dogs and off-leash readiness." schema={schema}>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{name:'Home',href:'/'},{name:'Dog Guides',href:'/dog-guides/'},{name:'Dog Recall Training'}]}/>
  <header class="page-head wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">DOG RECALL TRAINING HUB</p>
    <h1>Dog Recall Training for Real-World Distractions</h1>
    <p class="lede">Build a clear recall cue, protect it with sensible management, then train it on a long line around distance, distractions and changing environments instead of repeatedly testing the dog before the skill is ready.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="section section-tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head"><h2>A practical recall-training progression</h2><p>Outdoor recall becomes more useful when you can identify exactly what changed: distance, the value of the distraction, the environment, or the dog’s arousal.</p></div>
      <div class="hub-steps">
        <article><h3>Protect the cue</h3><p>Call once when success is plausible. Use a leash, long line, fence, or more distance to prevent repeated practice of ignoring the cue.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Change one variable</h3><p>Increase distance, distraction, or environmental difficulty separately so a failed repetition tells you something useful.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Pay for disengagement</h3><p>Reward the choice the dog actually made. Leaving a fresh scent trail, another dog, or wildlife at a trained distance may deserve more than an easy indoor return.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Earn freedom by context</h3><p>Judge readiness for the exact kind of place in front of you. Strong recall in one field does not automatically transfer to woodland, a beach, a park, or a trail.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">PRACTICAL GUIDES</p><h2>Start with the recall problem or distraction causing trouble</h2></div>
      <div class="grid-3">{guides.map(g => <GuideCard guide={g} base="/reliable-recall/" label="DOG RECALL TRAINING"/>)}</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-tint">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Go deeper with the complete proofing system</h2>
      <p><a href="/books/reliable-recall/"><em>Reliable Recall: From Long Line to Real-World Freedom</em></a> covers cue repair, reward strategy, indoor foundations, long-line handling, the Distance–Distraction–Environment proofing map, social and scent distractions, wildlife, emergency recall, generalization, environment-specific off-leash readiness, maintenance, troubleshooting, a 30-day plan, scenarios, and repeat-use logs and checkpoints.</p>
      <p>Recall is a safety skill, not a replacement for physical restraint where a mistake could have serious consequences. Roads, livestock, wildlife, cliffs, unsafe water, local leash rules, and uncontrolled public spaces can make a leash or long line the correct choice regardless of training progress.</p>
      <p>If another dog triggers fear, lunging, snapping, or intense distress rather than simple social excitement, use the <a href="/reactive-dog-walking/">Reactive Dog Training hub</a> and consider appropriate professional support. For dogs whose responsiveness changes as arousal rises more generally, the <a href="/over-aroused-dog/">Over-Aroused Dog hub</a> may also be useful.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
